L000696	\subsection*{Supplementary Text}
L000697	\subsubsection*{Enrichment of the animal}
L000698	The macaque monkeys were housed individually or in pairs at the primate facility of the University of Rochester, on a 12-hour light-dark cycle (light from 7 am to 7 pm). The enrichment includes toys and videos played on television during the day, selected to match monkeys' interests. They were provided with a nutritionally balanced diet, monitored by on-site veterinarians and animal facility staff. Their water intake was regulated, ensuring a minimum of 20 ml/kg/day during experimental sessions and 60 ml/kg/day on non-experimental days. Regular health assessments were performed by trained personnel, including onsite veterinarians and experienced laboratory members familiar with primate care.
L000699	
L000700	\subsubsection*{Array implantation surgery} \label{sec:supp_surgery}
L000701	Surgery was performed under isoflurane anesthesia using aseptic technique, with perioperative opioid analgesics and postoperative antibiotics. To expose the target cranial site, we incised the scalp or removed bone cement as necessary. A bone drill was used to create $\sim 20$~mm craniotomies.
L000702	
L000703	Implant location was confirmed via visual reference to sulcal landmarks: rostral to the lunate sulcus, caudal to the superior temporal sulcus, and dorsal to the ascending ramus of the inferior occipital sulcus. We then introduced a $\sim10$~mm durotomy, positioned the array and array connector (secured with cortical screws), and inserted the array to a depth of 1~mm using a pneumatic array inserter (Blackrock Microsystems).
L000704	
L000705	One reference wire was inserted under the dura, and another was inserted between the dura and the bone. The durotomy was then sutured closed and the dura was covered with surgical cellulose foam. We then sealed the craniotomy with bone cement (monkey G) or titanium straps secured with cortical screws (monkey R). Finally, the scalp was sutured closed.
L000706	
L000707	
L000708	\subsubsection*{Compute psychometric kernel} \label{sec:supp_psykernel}
L000709	To quantify the animals' behavioral strategy in each task, we followed prior work \cite{lange2023weak} and ran a logistic regression that predicts the animal's choice for each trial based on the empirical ``orientation energy" ($\mathbf{E}$) in the images.  $\mathbf{E}$ is an array with  dimensions trials ($t$) $\times$ orientations ($\theta$) $\times$ frames ($f$), where each entry, $e_{t,{\theta},f}$, was computed as the dot product between an orientation-dependent template ($\mathbf{M(\theta)}$) and the signal power of one frame in the Fourier domain ($||\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{I}_{t,f})||^2$):
L000710	\[e_{t,{\theta,}f} = \sum _{\rho,\phi}||\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{I}_{tf})||^2_{\rho\phi} \mathbf{M(\theta)}_{\rho\phi}\]
L000711	The orientation template is given by
L000712	\[M(\theta)_{\rho\phi} = Rice(\rho;\mu_\rho, \sigma_\rho) \mathbb{I}_{tf}\{|\theta - \phi| < 7.5\degree\}\]
L000713	where $Rice()$ denotes he density function of Rician distribution, and $\rho$, $\phi$ are the spatial frequency and orientation polar coordinates in the Fourier domain. $\mu_{\rho}$ and $\sigma_{\rho}$ represent the mean and standard deviation of spatial frequency, as empirically used for each subject (see \hyperlink{sec:Visual stimuli}{materials and methods}). We used 12 templates, with $\theta$ spaced from 0\degree\ to 165\degree\ in 15\degree\ increments.
L000714	
L000715	To standardize regressors, we generated zero-coherence stimuli with matched radius, spatial frequency, and spatial frequency standard deviation. All entries of $\mathbf{E}$ were $z$-scored with respect to mean and standard deviations of the orientation energy in zero-coherence stimuli, ensuring comparability across tasks and animals.
L000716	
L000717	The psychophysical kernel model included 31 parameters: the orientation kernel ($\mathbf{w}_{\theta}$) with 12 orientation weights, the temporal kernel ($\mathbf{w}_f$) with 16 frame weights (assuming independence between $\mathbf{w}_{\theta}$ and $\mathbf{w}_f$), a bias term ($w_b$), and two lapse parameters ($\lambda_l$ and $\lambda_h$). The model was defined as follows:
L000718	\begin{align*}
L000719	z_t &= w_b + \sum_{f,\theta} w_f w_\theta e_{f,\theta}\\
L000720	q_t &= \lambda_l + (1 - \lambda_l - \lambda_h) \frac{1}{1 + e^{-z_t}}\\
L000721	\text{negative log likelihood} &= -\sum_{\substack{t \\ c_t = +1}} \log(q_t) -\sum_{\substack{t \\ c_t = -1}} \log(1 - q_t)
L000722	\end{align*}
L000723	where $c_t$ is the animal's choice on trial $t$, and $q_t$ is the predicted probability of choosing the ``positive" orientation (0\degree\ or 45\degree) on trial $t$.
L000724	
L000725	The prior on $w_b$ was Gaussian, while lapse parameters had an exponential prior. To prevent overfitting, we implemented smoothing priors for $\mathbf{w_\theta}$ and $\mathbf{w_f}$:
L000726	\begin{equation*}
L000727	\begin{split}
L000728	\text{negative log prior} & = \frac{\text{ori}_\text{ar2}}{2} \mathbf{w_\theta}^{\top}\mathbf{AR2_{ori}}\mathbf{w_\theta} +
L000729	                \frac{\text{time}_\text{ar0}}{2} \mathbf{w_f}^{\top}\mathbf{w_f} +
L000730	                \frac{\text{time}_\text{ar2}}{2} \mathbf{w_f}^{\top}\mathbf{AR2_{time}}\mathbf{w_f} \\ & +
L000731	                 \frac{\text{bias}_\text{var}}{2} w_b^2 + \frac{\lambda_l + \lambda_h}{\text{lapse}_\text{mean}}
L000732	\end{split}
L000733	\end{equation*}
L000734	where hyperparameters were set as:
L000735	\begin{equation*}
L000736	[\text{ori}_\text{ar2},\text{time}_\text{ar0},\text{time}_\text{ar2},\text{bias}_\text{var},\text{lapse}_\text{mean}] = [0.5,6,60,0.15,0.06]
L000737	\end{equation*}
L000738	Here, $\mathbf{AR2_{ori}}$ and $\mathbf{AR2_{time}}$ denote the second-order finite-difference matrix with kernel $[1,-2,1]$.
L000739	
L000740	Parameter inference was performed using \textsc{MATLAB} built-in optimization function \textsc{fmincon}, which minimized the negative log-posterior via the sequential quadratic programming algorithm. Lapse parameters were constrained between 0 and 1, and temporal kernel parameters were constrained to be positive. The L-2 norm of $\mathbf{w_\theta}$ was constrained to 1.
L000741	
L000742	We employed a two-step fitting procedure: first, the temporal kernel was estimated while assuming fixed orientation kernel weights ($w_{\theta} = +1$ for $\theta = 0\degree\ \text{or } 45\degree$; $w_{\theta} = -1$ for $\theta = 90\degree\ \text{or } 135\degree$). The resulting temporal weights were then used to initialize the joint fitting of spatial and temporal kernels. Initial values for $\mathbf{w_\theta}$ and $w_b$ were set to zero, while $\lambda_l$ and $\lambda_h$ were initialized at 0.01.
L000743	
L000744	
L000745	
L000746	\subsubsection*{Behavioral Fisher information} \label{sec:supp_behavFisher}
L000747	To directly compare behavior with neural Fisher information using equivalent units, we computed ``behavioral Fisher information" ($\Ibehav$). At each coherence level $x$, we assumed that the subjects represented each orientation with a Gaussian distribution characterized by a mean $\mu$ and standard deviation $\sigma$ (shared between the two orientations), and made decision based on a criteria $c$.
L000748	
L000749	The probability of choosing the ``negative" ($\theta = 90\degree\ \text{or } 135\degree$) orientation when the stimulus orientation was ``negative'' or ``positive'' ($\theta = 90\degree\ \text{or } 135\degree$) can be computed from the behavioral data, where $y$ denotes choice and $s$ denotes stimulus):
L000750	\[p(y = -1|s = -x) = \frac{\sum_{t}\mathbb{I}\{y_t = -1, s_t = -x\}}{\sum_{t}\mathbb{I}\{s_t = -x\}}\]
L000751	\[p(y = -1|s = x) = \frac{\sum_{t}\mathbb{I}\{y_t = -1, s_t = x\}}{\sum_{t}\mathbb{I}\{s_t = x\}}\]
L000752	Then, we transform these probabilities into $z$-scores:
L000753	\[z^- = \Phi^{-1}(p(y = -1|s = -x)), \quad
L000754	z^+ = \Phi^{-1}(p(y = -1|s = x))\]
L000755	where $\Phi^{-1}$ represents the inverse of the cumulative function of a Gaussian distribution with mean $\mu$ and variance $\sigma^2$.
L000756	
L000757	Since the $z$-score is given by
L000758	\[z = \frac{c - \mu}{\sigma}\]
L000759	we obtain the following equations:
L000760	\[
L000761	\sigma = \frac{\mu + c}{z^-}, \quad \sigma = \frac{\mu + c}{z^+}.
L000762	\]
L000763	Solving for $c$ and $\sigma$, we get
L000764	\[c = \mu \frac{z^+ - z^-}{z^+ + z^-}, \quad
L000765	\sigma = \frac{-2\mu}{z^+ - z^-}\]
L000766	We also define the behavioral $f^\prime$ as
L000767	\[f^\prime = \frac{2\mu}{2x} = \frac{\mu}{x}\]
L000768	Thus, behavioral Fisher information is given by
L000769	\begin{equation}
L000770	I_{\rm{behav}} = \frac{{f^\prime}^2}{\sigma^2} = \frac{(z^+ - z^-)^2}{4x^2}
L000771	\label{eq_Ibehav}
L000772	\end{equation}
L000773	Although we did not have direct access to the subjects' internal representations of stimuli ($\mu$, $\sigma$) or their decision criteria ($c$), these unknown variables canceled out in the process of computing $\Ibehav$.
L000774	
L000775	We used Eq.~\ref{eq_Ibehav} to compute $\Ibehav$ for each coherence level. The overall $\Ibehav$ for a session was calculated as a weighted average across coherence levels, with the number of trials serving as weights.
L000776	
L000777	
L000778	\subsubsection*{Computing linear Fisher Information} \label{sec:supp_lisherfisherinfo}
L000779	We estimated linear Fisher Information using analytical methods using bias correction \cite{kanitscheider2015measuring}. That work assumed equal trial counts for both stimulus conditions, but in our empirical data, animals performed different numbers of trials per condition. We therefore modified the formulas to account for different trial counts, $T_1$ and $T_2$, corresponding to the two orientations being discriminated.
L000780	
L000781	\paragraph{Bias-corrected estimation of $\Ir$}
L000782	On each trial, a stimulus from one of two classes (positive or negative orientation) was presented. Let $\mathbf{r}_t^{+}$ and $\mathbf{r}_t^{-}$ denote the population responses to positive and negative stimuli on trial $t$ respectively.  We follow prior work \cite{kanitscheider2015measuring} in assuming (1) Gaussian variability, (2) equal covariance across conditions, and (3) locally linear tuning curves with respect to signal level. Model responses can be written as:
L000783	\[\mathbf{r_t^{\pm}}  \sim \mathcal{N}\left( \vec{f}(\theta \pm d\theta), \vec{\Sigma} \right) \approx \mathcal{N} \left(\vec{f}(\theta) \pm d\theta \vec{f^{\prime}}(\theta), \vec{\Sigma} \right)\]
L000784	In our task, the two orientations were always 90 degrees apart, $\theta$ denotes signal level of stimulus,  which controlled the discrimination difficulty (See \hyperref[sec:Exp_VisualStimuli]{materials and methods}). The tuning curves were assumed linear for signal levels close to zero (we constrained our analysis to trials with $|\theta| \leq 20\%$). $T_1$ denotes number of trials for the positive class and $T_2$ for the negative classes. The empirical mean and covariance are given by:
L000785	\[\vec{\mu^{+}} = \frac{1}{T_1} \sum_{t=1}^{T_1}\mathbf{r_t^{+}}, \quad
L000786	\vec{\mu^{-}} = \frac{1}{T_2} \sum_{t=1}^{T_2}\mathbf{r_t^{-}}\]
L000787	\[\vec{S}=\frac{\sum_{t=1}^{T_1}\left(\mathbf{r_t^{+}}-\vec{\mu}^{+}\right)\left(\mathbf{r_t^{+}}-
L000788	\vec{\mu}^{+}\right)^{\top}+\sum_{t=1}^{T_2}\left(\mathbf{r_t^{-}}-\vec{\mu}^{-}\right)\left(\mathbf{r_t^{-}}-\vec{\mu}^{-}\right)^{\top}}{T_1+T_2-2}\]
L000789	The sampling distributions of the empirical mean of two stimuli are:
L000790	\begin{gather*}
L000791	\vec{\mu}^+ \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\vec{f}(\theta) + \frac{d\theta}{2} \vec{f}^{\prime}(\theta), \frac{\vec{\Sigma}}{T_1}\right) \\
L000792	\vec{\mu}^- \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\vec{f}(\theta) -\frac{d\theta}{2}  \vec{f}^{\prime}(\theta), \frac{\vec{\Sigma}}{T_2}\right)
L000793	\end{gather*}
L000794	From
L000795	\begin{equation}
L000796	\frac{d\vec{\mu}}{d\theta} = \frac{\vec{\mu}^+ - \vec{\mu}^-}{d\theta}
L000797	\label{eq_dmu}
L000798	\end{equation}
L000799	it follows that the unbiased estimator in Eq. \eqref{eq_dmu} is sampled from:
L000800	\begin{equation}
L000801	\frac{d\vec{\mu}}{d\theta} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\vec{f}^{\prime}(\theta), \frac{T_1+T_2}{T_1 T_2}  \frac{\vec{\Sigma}}{d \theta^2}\right)
L000802	\label{eq_dmu_sample}
L000803	\end{equation}
L000804	
L000805	We assume equal covariance $\vec{\Sigma}$ under two stimulus conditions, so $\mathbf{r_t^{+}}-\vec{\mu}^{+}$ and $\mathbf{r_t^{-}}-\vec{\mu}^{-}$ are drawn from an N-variate normal distribution
L000806	\[\mathbf{r_t^{+}}-\vec{\mu}^{+} \sim \mathcal{N}_N(\mathbf{0}, \vec{\Sigma}) \]
L000807	\[\mathbf{r_t^{-}}-\vec{\mu}^{-} \sim \mathcal{N}_N(\mathbf{0}, \vec{\Sigma}) \]
L000808	
L000809	Therefore, $S$ follows Wishart's distribution
L000810	\[\vec{S} \sim W_N\left(\frac{\vec{\Sigma}}{T_1+T_2-2} , T_1+T_2-2\right)\]
L000811	The expectation of the inverse of $\vec{S}$ is:
L000812	\begin{equation}
L000813	\left\langle \vec{S^{-1}}\right\rangle=\frac{T_1+T_2-2}{T_1+T_2-N-3} \vec{\Sigma}^{-1}
L000814	\label{eq_S_inverse_expectation}
L000815	\end{equation}
L000816	The naive estimator of linear Fisher information is defined as
L000817	\[\hat{I}_{\text {naive }}=\frac{d \vec{\mu}^{\top}}{d \theta} \vec{S^{-1}} \frac{d \vec{\mu}}{d \theta}\]
L000818	Following \cite{kanitscheider2015measuring}, the expectation value of this naive estimator is:
L000819	\begin{equation}
L000820	\left\langle \hat{I}_{\text {naive}} \right\rangle = \left\langle\frac{d \vec{\mu}^{\top}}{d \theta} \vec{S^{-1}} \frac{d\vec{\mu}}{d \theta}\right\rangle  =\operatorname{Tr}\left(\left\langle\frac{d\vec{\mu}}{d\theta} \frac{d\vec{\mu}^{\top}}{d\theta} \vec{S^{-1}}\right\rangle\right)  =\operatorname{Tr}\left(\left\langle\frac{d\vec{\mu}}{d\theta} \frac{d\vec{\mu}^{\top}}{d \theta}\right\rangle \left\langle \vec{S^{-1}}\right\rangle\right)
L000821	\label{eq_I_naive_expecation}
L000822	\end{equation}
L000823	Substituting Eq. \eqref{eq_dmu_sample} and Eq. \eqref{eq_S_inverse_expectation} into Eq. \eqref{eq_I_naive_expecation}, we obtain:
L000824	\begin{equation}
L000825	\begin{split}
L000826	\left\langle \hat{I}_\text{naive}\right\rangle & =\frac{T_1+T_2-2}{T_1+T_2-N-3} \operatorname{Tr}\left(\left(\vec{f}^{\prime} \vec{f}^{\prime \top}+\frac{T_1+T_2}{T_1 T_2} \frac{\vec{\Sigma}}{d\theta^2}\right) \vec{\Sigma}^{-1}\right) \\ & =\frac{T_1+T_2-2}{T_1+T_2-N-3}\left(I+\frac{T_1+T_2}{T_1 T_2} \frac{N}{d\theta^2}\right)
L000827	\end{split}
L000828	\end{equation}
L000829	Therefore, we can solve the bias-corrected estimator of linear Fisher information:
L000830	\begin{equation}
L000831	\hat{I}_\text{bc}=\hat{I}_\text{naive} \frac{T_1+T_2-N-3}{T_1+T_2-2}-\frac{\left(T_1+T_2\right) N}{T_1 T_2 d\theta^2}
L000832	\label{eq_I_estimate}
L000833	\end{equation}
L000834	
L000835	
L000836	\paragraph{Bias-corrected estimation of $\Is$}
L000837	
L000838	We also need to derive the unbiased estimator for $\Is$ to compute  $\Idelta$, the difference between $\Is$ and $\Ir$. $\Is$ can be treated as the sum of linear Fisher information from individual neurons, assuming they are independent.
L000839	\[I_{\text {naive, shuffle }}= \sum_{i=1}^N  I_i= \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\left(\frac{d\mu_i}{ d\theta}\right)^2}{s_i^2}\]
L000840	The distributions of empirical mean responses are identical to Eq. \eqref{eq_dmu_sample}.The distributions and expectations for each neuron's variance can are:
L000841	\[s_i^2 \sim\left(\frac{\sigma_i^2}{T_1+T_2-2}, T_1+T_2-2\right)\]
L000842	with the expectation of the inverse of variances:
L000843	\begin{equation}
L000844	\left\langle\frac{1}{s_i^2}\right\rangle=\frac{T_1+T_2-2}{\left(T_1+T_2-4\right) \sigma_i^2}
L000845	\end{equation}
L000846	Again, following \cite{kanitscheider2015measuring}, the expectation of the naive $\Is$ is
L000847	\begin{equation}
L000848	\begin{split}
L000849	\left\langle \hat{I}_\text{naive,shuffle}\right\rangle  & =\sum_{i=1}^N\left\langle\left(\frac{d\mu_i}{d \theta}\right)^2\right\rangle\left\langle\frac{1}{s_i^2}\right\rangle \\
L000850	& =\sum_{i=1}^N\left(f_i^{\prime 2}+\frac{T_1+T_2}{T_1 T_2} \frac{\sigma_i^2}{d\theta^2}\right) \frac{T_1+T_2-2}{(T_1+T_2-4)\sigma_i^2} \\
L000851	& =\frac{T_1+T_2-2}{T_1+T_2-4}\left(\Is +\frac{T_1+T_2}{T_1 T_2} \frac{N}{d \theta^2}\right)
L000852	\end{split}
L000853	\end{equation}
L000854	Thus, the bias-corrected estimator of $\Is$ is
L000855	\begin{equation}
L000856	\hat{I}_\text{bc,shuffle}=\hat{I}_\text{naive,shuffle} \frac{T_1+T_2-4}{T_1+T_2-2}-\frac{\left(T_1+T_2\right) N}{T_1 T_2 d \theta^2}
L000857	\label{eq_I_shuffle_estimate}
L000858	\end{equation}
L000859	
L000860	
L000861	\paragraph{Variance of the bias-corrected linear Fisher information}
L000862	To quantify the uncertainty of Fisher information estimation for individual sessions, we employed the analytical method of \cite{kanitscheider2015measuring} to calculate the variance of bias-corrected Fisher information, making minor adjustments to account for different trial numbers across conditions. The variance variance of $I_{bc}$ is given by
L000863	\begin{equation}
L000864	\operatorname{var}\hat{I_\text{bc}} = (\alpha+2\beta) I^2 +
L000865	                (6\alpha+12\beta+4)\gamma I +
L000866	                (3\alpha+6\beta+2)\gamma^2 N
L000867	\label{eq_varI_estimate}
L000868	\end{equation}
L000869	
L000870	where
L000871	\[\alpha=\frac{2}{(v-p)(v-p-3)}, \quad \beta = \frac{v-p-1}{(v-p)(v-p-3)}\]
L000872	
L000873	\[\gamma = \frac{T_1 + T_2}{T_1 T_2 d\theta^2}, \quad v =T_1+T_2-2, \quad p = N\]
L000874	
L000875	Next, we compute the variance of bias-corrected $I_\text{shuffle}$. Since $\hat{I}_\text{shuffle}= \sum_{i=1}^N  \hat{I}_i $, we have
L000876	\begin{equation}
L000877	\operatorname{var}\hat{I}_\text{shuffle}=\sum_{i=1}^N  \operatorname{var}\hat{I}_i
L000878	\label{eq_varI_shuffle_estimate_supp}
L000879	\end{equation}
L000880	where $\operatorname{var}\hat{I}_i$ can be computed with Eq. \eqref{eq_varI_estimate} plugging in $p=1$.
L000881	
